{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>They have dealt corruptly with Him, not His children, their blemish.</b> In the Yalkut, parashat Ha'azinu (32, 992), it concludes: 'Look at the heavens that I created to serve you — perhaps they changed their order…' and 'how much more so you, who have reward and loss and are compassionate toward your sons and your daughters — how much more and how much more should you not change your order.' But who told the author of this midrash to mention daughters or sons in this a fortiori argument? Above, in parashat Nitzavim (30:19), I explained that he learned this from what is written there: ״הַעִידֹתִי בָכֶם אֶת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֶת הָאָרֶץ״, and it is written immediately after: ״לְמַעַן תִּחְיֶה אַתָּה וְזַרְעֶךָ״ — and why did he mention his seed in this admonition more than in all the other admonitions? Rather, thus He said to him: if you will not act for the sake of your own life, act please for the sake of the life of your seed, for you are by nature compassionate toward son and daughter. And so in this parashah as well: after he said ״הַאֲזִינוּ הַשָּׁמַיִם״ and so forth, he said ״שִׁחֵת לוֹ לֹא בָּנָיו מוּמָם״. First he said ״שִׁחֵת לוֹ״ — that the sinner and the rebellious son destroys himself — and not only himself, but he is not even compassionate toward the lives of his children, as if they were not his children; and this is their great blemish. For sometimes the craving of the moment presses him so that he does not reflect on the end when the punishment will come. But his children — what have they sinned? For they have no share in this pleasure, but they do have a share in the punishment." "And this is what is said immediately after: ״וַיַּרְא ה׳ וַיִּנְאָץ מִכַּעַס בָּנָיו וּבְנוֹתָיו״. For as to that which he was not compassionate about himself — that is not such a great provocation, because ״יִשְׁמַן יְשֻׁרוּן וַיִּבְעָט שָׁמַנְתָּ עָבִיתָ״ and so forth, meaning that the pleasure of the moment pressed him. But his sons and his daughters, who do not have even the pleasure of the moment — how could he not have compassion on them, to bring them into the net of that punishment? This is the thing that most provokes the Holy One, blessed be He. This is what is said immediately after ״שָׁמַנְתָּ עָבִיתָ״ and so forth: ״וַיַּרְא ה׳ וַיִּנְאָץ מִכַּעַס בָּנָיו וּבְנוֹתָיו״ — that HaShem spurns him because he provokes his sons and his daughters. Therefore these two acts of destruction are both mentioned here. And He magnified far more the destruction that he causes his children than the destruction he brings upon his own soul, for thus it is said: ״וַיַּרְא ה׳ וַיִּנְאָץ מִכַּעַס בָּנָיו וּבְנוֹתָיו״, and here ״שִׁחֵת לוֹ לֹא בָּנָיו מוּמָם״ — even though he destroyed himself, nevertheless this blemish within him is not as great as the blemish he inflicts upon his children. For it seems to Him as if they were not his children — this is the essence of their blemish." "Another matter: ״בָּנָיו מוּמָם״ — this is the essence of their blemish, that they are the children of HaShem, blessed be He, and they did not look to the Rock from whom they were hewn. For the Rock — His work is perfect, and it was incumbent upon them to walk in the ways of their Father who is in heaven, as it is said (Deuteronomy 13:5): ״אַחֲרֵי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם תֵּלֵכוּ״ — walk after His ways, as it is said: ״הֲלֹא הוּא אָבִיךָ קָּנֶךָ״, and it was incumbent upon you to walk in His ways." "Another matter: ״בָּנָיו״ alludes to his deeds, which are called a person's progeny — the progeny of the righteous being good deeds, and the deeds of the wicked, as it is said (Psalms 7:15): ״וְהָרָה עָמָל וְיָלַד שָׁקֶר״. And the wicked person never acknowledges the truth, and with all his evil progeny that he produces he will say 'they are not his children.' And this is the essence of the blemish within them — that he does not hold himself guilty in his soul for all his evil progeny, but denies it, saying 'they are not his children; I did not beget them.' And the reason for this is that they are ״דּוֹר עִקֵּשׁ וּפְתַלְתֹּל״. For from the aspect of his being a דּוֹר עִקֵּשׁ — a crooked generation — he never acknowledges the truth, and says to every reprover: ״לֹא פָעַלְתִּי אָוֶן״. A second reason: from the aspect of his being one who transgresses a sin and repeats it and becomes habituated to doing it many times, until the matter becomes for him as though permitted (Yoma 86a) — and this is what he said: ״וּפְתַלְתֹּל״ — like a threefold cord: one who transgresses and repeats and transgresses a third time, it becomes for him as though permitted, and he never turns back from his way."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The verse citation for the Yalkut Shim'oni is given as (לב תתקמב), which resolves to section 32, entry 992 in standard numbering; rendered as 32, 992."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:04.481590167Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}